Consider the following two tables and four queries in SQL.
\texttt{Book (isbn, bname)}
\texttt{Stock (isbn, copies)}

Query 1: \texttt{SELECT B.isbn, S.copies FROM Book B INNER JOIN Stock S ON B.isbn = S.isbn;}
Query 2: \texttt{SELECT B.isbn, S.copies FROM Book B LEFT OUTER JOIN Stock S ON B.isbn = S.isbn;}
Query 3: \texttt{SELECT B.isbn, S.copies FROM Book B RIGHT OUTER JOIN Stock S ON B.isbn = S.isbn;}
Query 4: \texttt{SELECT B.isbn, S.copies FROM Book B FULL OUTER JOIN Stock S ON B.isbn = S.isbn;}

Which one of the following statements is CORRECT?

Consider two relations describing $teams$ and $players$ in a sports league:
\begin{itemize}
\item $teams(tid, tname)$: $tid, tname$ are team-id and team-name, respectively
\item $players(pid, pname, tid)$: $pid, pname$, and $tid$ denote player-id, player-name and the team-id of the player, respectively
\end{itemize}
Which ONE of the following tuple relational calculus queries returns the name of the players who play for the team having $tname$ as 'MI'?

questions.question_text:
Consider the following relational schema along with all the functional dependencies
that hold on them.
R1(A, B, C, D, E): { 𝐷→𝐸, 𝐸𝐴→𝐵, 𝐸𝐵→𝐶}
R2(A, B, C, D): { 𝐴→𝐷, 𝐴→𝐵, 𝐶→𝐴}
Which of the following statement(s) is/are TRUE?

options_latex.A:
SELECT S.name
FROM Students S
WHERE EXISTS (SELECT * FROM Enrolled E WHERE E.courseno = 1470 AND E.rollno = S.rollno);

options_latex.B:
SELECT S.name
FROM Students S
WHERE SIZEOF (SELECT * FROM Enrolled E WHERE E.courseno = 1470 AND E.rollno = S.rollno) > 0;

options_latex.C:
SELECT S.name
FROM Students S
WHERE 0 < (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Enrolled E WHERE E.courseno = 1470 AND E.rollno = S.rollno);

options_latex.D:
SELECT S.name
FROM Students S NATURAL JOIN Enrolled E
WHERE E.courseno = 1470;
